// ActivityCommitBucket is one (local day, local hour, linked-agent set) bucket of
// the caller's deduped commits: the commit count, and the RAW agent spellings of
// every checkpoint trailered onto those commits.
//
// Agents is empty for a commit no checkpoint links — which the series reports as
// its own "none" series rather than an absence, so the empty set has to survive
// the read and cannot be folded away here. The caller maps the spellings through
// getAgentID and picks the series label (commitSeries).
type ActivityCommitBucket struct {
	Day     string
	Hour    int
	Agents  []string
	Commits int
}

// ActivityCommitBuckets returns the caller's deduped commits bucketed by local
// day, local hour and the agent set of their linked checkpoints. tz is an IANA
// name, as for ActivityAgentBuckets.
//
// Commits dedup on (origin_key, sha) — the commit's LOGICAL identity, since a
// mirror's placements each report it — matching the row fold's key. Checkpoints
// join on the same pair, which is why one scoped CTE covers both types: they
// share every predicate but `type`, so the access gate and window bind once.
func (s *UserStore) ActivityCommitBuckets(ctx context.Context, q RecapQuery, tz string) ([]ActivityCommitBucket, error) {
	sqlStr, args, ok, err := activityCommitBucketsQuery(q, tz)
	if err != nil || !ok {
		return nil, err
	}
	rows, err := s.pool.Query(ctx, sqlStr, args...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("store: activity commit buckets: %w", err)
	}
	defer rows.Close()
	var out []ActivityCommitBucket
	for rows.Next() {
		var b ActivityCommitBucket
		if err := rows.Scan(&b.Day, &b.Hour, &b.Agents, &b.Commits); err != nil {
			return nil, fmt.Errorf("store: scan activity commit buckets: %w", err)
		}
		out = append(out, b)
	}
	return out, rows.Err()
}

// activityCommitBucketsQuery builds ActivityCommitBuckets' statement together
// with its binds. Split out so the plan-shape regression test EXPLAINs the
// statement AND the parameters that ship — pinning only the SQL text would let
// the bind order drift and leave the test asserting on a plan nobody runs. ok is
// false when the caller has no accessible repos, as spineBase reports it.
//
// NOT MATERIALIZED is load-bearing, not decoration. A CTE referenced twice is
// materialised by default, which spools every windowed row of both types into a
// tuplestore before each arm filters the other type back out. Inlined it costs
// no more than writing the two arms out separately: over a window of 23k
// commits and 23k checkpoints, 42-44ms materialised against 28-31ms inlined and
// 30-32ms for two hand-written arms.
//
// Measure any change to this query against a window holding BOTH types. The
// perf fixture seeds checkpoints only, and with no commit rows the join
// short-circuits: every shape looks single-digit there (1-13ms) and the
// materialised penalty reads as ~10x rather than the ~1.4x it is in practice.
//
// c: one row per logical commit, newest reporter winning, as dedupCheckpointRows
// does, renaming its columns so `scoped`'s bare names stay unambiguous in the
// outer join. links: the distinct agents per (origin_key, sha) over the whole
// checkpoint arm. The LEFT JOIN keeps only the groups a counted commit names,
// and gives an unlinked commit an empty agent set.
//
// links deliberately does NOT join c. It used to, to make the aggregation scale
// with commits rather than checkpoints — only a sha that HAS a counted commit
// can label one. That correlated join is what made this query the /me/activity
// tail: joining an aggregate back onto a derived CTE is unestimable, and it sat
// downstream of an access gate the planner already misjudges by ~50x.
//
// `(repo_id = ANY(...) OR origin_key = ANY(...))` (baseFilter) reads as highly
// selective to the planner, but those arrays ARE the account's own repos, so at
// runtime it discards almost nothing — it is a revocation filter, not a scoping
// one. Measured on prod us-east-2 (3 origins, 13,292 rows in a 30-day window):
// base scan estimated 218 rows against ~11,102 actual, c estimated 200, and the
// join onto it estimated 1 — so the planner chose a nested loop over c, whose
// inner index scan can only use (account_id, origin_key) because sha is not in
// that index. ~11k iterations x ~1/3 of the account's rows is ~49M row
// examinations, i.e. the 11.4-12.0s p95.
//
// Note the direction: FEWER origins is worse, because origin_key is the only
// index condition the inner scan gets. Every request over 5s carried 3 or 4
// accessible repos, not many.
//
// Aggregating the checkpoint arm unconditionally costs a plain GROUP BY the
// planner can estimate, and removes the nested loop it could pick. The premise
// the old shape optimised for is also inverted on exactly these accounts: they
// carry more commits than checkpoints (11,102 against 2,190 above).
//
// Every other spine read shares this gate but is scan-aggregate, where a bad
// estimate costs a spill rather than a quadratic join, so none of them needs the
// same treatment. Making the gate itself estimable — driving from the access set
// rather than filtering against it — is the deeper fix, and a separate change.
